<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sukerchakia misl</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sukerchakia misl was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-confederacy">Sikh confederacies</a> of 18th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, based at Gujranwala and founded by Charat Singh. Under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-singh">Maha Singh</a> it grew strong, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> used it as the base for capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1799 and absorbing the other misls into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>, of which he was proclaimed maharaja in 1801.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sukerchakia-misl.jpg" alt="Sukerchakia misl" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Navtej Heer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NORTHERN_INDIA_1780_by_hellbat.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from suker, small or narrow, and chak, a petty tract of land, and was first given to land owned and built upon by Budh Singh, an ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>. The ruling family were Jat Sikhs, and their earliest reliably known ancestor was Kalu, a Jat of the Warraich clan who died around 1488. His descendant Kiddoh settled around 1555 at the village of Sukerchak, about 3 kilometres from Gujranwala, from which the misl took its name. Budda, born in 1670, was baptised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> himself as Budh Singh and became a warrior of high repute.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Budh Singh's son Naudh Singh built a small fort at Sukerchak and gathered a band of 30 horsemen to defend the district against invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>. In 1730 his men joined the Faizalpuria Misl of Nawab Kapur Singh, and he grew wealthy by plundering Afghan caravans. In 1748 a resolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarbat-khalsa">Sarbat Khalsa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a> reorganised the scattered jathas into misls, and the Sukerchakia Misl took shape from it. Naudh died in 1752.</p><p>His eldest son Charat Singh made his name in campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Abdali</a>, broke away from the Singhpuria Misl and extended his rule to Rohtas, Chakwal, Pind Dadan Khan and Wazirabad. He strengthened his position by marriage alliances, giving daughters to leaders of the Bhangi misl and marrying his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-singh">Maha Singh</a> to a daughter of Jai Singh Mann of Mughalchak. Maha Singh, who died in 1792, expanded the misl further, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, its last leader, united the misls into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A misl was a grouping with its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> militia, or jatha. Under Charat Singh only men already baptised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> could join, and the number of horsemen rose to around 150. The headquarters moved from Sukerchak to Gujranwala after Charat married Desan Kaur, daughter of the powerful sardar Amir Singh of Gujranwala. The misl held the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> rivers south of the Bhangis, and fought at Sialkot and Gujranwala in 1761 and at Gujrat in 1797.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sukerchakia Misl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leading misls included the Bhangi, Ahluwalia, Ramgarhia and <b>Sukerchakia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/sikh-misls">Sikh Misls</a></li></ul></section>
